Check out our [Next.js deployment documentation](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/deploying) for more details.

## Group-buy receipt storage

Group-buy payment proofs use the Google account connected from **Admin → Settings → Group-buy receipt folder**. The connection requests the limited `drive.file` scope and saves its refresh token encrypted with `AUTH_SECRET`. PBC's existing receipt upload still uses its current service-account flow.

1. In Google Cloud Console, enable **Google Drive API** and **Google Picker API**.
2. Create a web OAuth client in the same Google Cloud project as the Picker API key. Add `https://admin-pbc.vercel.app/api/admin/google-drive/callback` as an authorized redirect URI. For local development, also add `http://localhost:3000/api/admin/google-drive/callback`.
3. Create an API key for Google Picker. Restrict it to the admin site's domain and `https://docs.google.com/*`; restrict APIs to Google Picker API and Drive API.
4. Set `GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET`, `NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_PICKER_API_KEY`, and `NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_PICKER_APP_ID` in the admin-pbc Vercel project, then redeploy.
5. In Admin Settings, connect the Google account that owns the order Drive folder. Select the main order folder; SellerHub creates a **Tindahan ni Tinapa Group Buy Receipts** subfolder inside it. You can also select that subfolder directly if it already exists.

The app stores the selected folder and encrypted refresh token in `app_settings`. It accesses only the selected folder and files the app creates there. Group-buy checkout cannot save an order until this connection and folder are ready.
Keep the OAuth consent screen in production mode. Google test-mode refresh tokens for Drive access expire after seven days. Keep `AUTH_SECRET` stable; rotating it requires reconnecting Drive.
